/S /P
/P 1134 0 R
/E (38. Why is Hold Fixing Done After Setup Fixing?)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
680 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1135 0 R
/E ( Setup fixing involves reducing path delays, which can worsen hold time. Hold fixing adds buffers to short paths, ensuring hold violations are corrected without affecting setup.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
681 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1136 0 R
/E (39. What is a Timing Arc?)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
682 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1137 0 R
/E (A timing arc defines how delay propagates inside a cell \(e.g., input to output delays in standard cells\). It is modeled in Liberty \(.lib\) files.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
683 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1138 0 R
/E (40. What is the purpose of Multicycle Paths in STA?)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
684 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1139 0 R
/E (Multicycle paths allow data to be captured after multiple clock cycles, reducing setup constraints and enabling longer delay paths without violations.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
685 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1140 0 R
/E (41. What is the difference between Ideal Clock and Propagated Clock in STA?)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
686 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1141 0 R
/E (Ideal Clock: No propagation delay is considered; arrival time is manually defined.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
687 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1142 0 R
/E (Propagated Clock: Actual clock network delays are considered during timing analysis.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
