/P 1145 0 R
/E (43. What is the difference between Latch-Based and Flip-Flop-Based STA?)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
692 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1146 0 R
/E (Flip-Flop STA: Analyzed using edge-triggered constraints \(setup/hold\).)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
693 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1147 0 R
/E (Latch-Based STA: Uses level-sensitive analysis, making it more complex due to timing window dependencies.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
694 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1148 0 R
/E (44. How does clock uncertainty impact high-speed processor design?)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
695 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1149 0 R
/E (Clock uncertainty \(caused by jitter, skew, and margin variations\) affects timing closure in high-speed processors. To mitigate its impact, techniques like clock gating, skew balancing, and on-chip PLL compensation are used. In multi-GHz processors, even a 10ps jitter can cause timing violations, leading to setup failures in critical paths.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
696 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1150 0 R
/E (45. Why is OCV \(On-Chip Variation\) more critical in 7nm and below technologies?)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
697 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1151 0 R
/E (At sub-7nm nodes, manufacturing variations \(PVT: Process, Voltage, Temperature\) become dominant, causing timing fluctuations. This leads to increased setup and hold margin mismatches. Adaptive voltage scaling \(AVS\) and dynamic OCV compensation \(LVF models\) are used to counter these variations dynamically in modern chip designs.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
698 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 211 0 R
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
699 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1152 0 R
/E (46. Explain how multi-corner, multi-mode \(MCMM\) analysis ensures reliable chip operation.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
700 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1153 0 R
/E (MCMM ensures a chip functions correctly under all scenarios \(fast/slow silicon, high/low temperature, different functional modes\). Without MCMM, a chip optimized only for typical conditions might fail in worst-case corners \(e.g., low voltage + high temperature\).)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
701 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1154 0 R
/E (Example: A mobile processor operates in turbo mode \(high frequency, high power\) and power-saving mode \(low frequency, low power\). MCMM analysis ensures both modes meet timing.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
702 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1155 0 R
/E (47. How does Metal Stack Selection Affect Timing Closure in ASIC Design?)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
703 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1156 0 R
/E (Different metal layers have different RC parasitics. High-speed signals should use upper metal layers \(M8, M9\) for lower resistance, while power and ground should use wider metal layers for efficient distribution. Incorrect metal selection can lead to higher delay, impacting setup slack and causing hold timing failures.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
704 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 212 0 R
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
