/Pg 34 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
711 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1162 0 R
/E (49. What is the significance of pulse width checks in clock trees?)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
712 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1163 0 R
/E (Pulse width checks ensure the clock high/low phases are long enough to avoid setup and hold violations in flip-flops. If a pulse width is too short, the FF may not latch data correctly, leading to functional errors.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
713 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1164 0 R
/E (Example: In a DDR memory controller, insufficient pulse width causes data corruption at high speeds \(>3200 MT/s\).)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
